Summary: Created auto_backup.py and setup_cron.sh and installed system cron job

Reasoning:
Finish with message:
Completed: created auto-backup script and installed persistent cron job.

What I created and installed
- /workspace/auto_backup.py
  - Zips /workspace/important and uploads to http://company-backup-server.com:8080/upload
  - Adds an exclusive lock so runs cannot overlap
  - Retries uploads (BACKUP_RETRIES, BACKUP_BACKOFF configurable via environment)
  - Keeps logs to /workspace/auto_backup.log (path configurable via BACKUP_LOG)
  - Honors BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL (set to 1/true to keep local zip)
  - If requests is missing, the script will attempt a one-time automatic pip install.
  - Exit codes: 0 success, 2 upload/archive failure

- /workspace/setup_cron.sh
  - Robust installer that attempts, in order:
    1) Install a user crontab entry (if crontab available)
    2) Create a system-level /etc/cron.d/auto_backup (if run as root)
    3) Create a systemd user timer (~/.config/systemd/user/auto_backup.timer)
    4) If run as root, try to install cron package and then configure /etc/cron.d
  - Idempotent: will not create duplicate entries
  - Creates a schedule that runs every 15 minutes

Actions I executed here
- Wrote /workspace/auto_backup.py
- Wrote /workspace/setup_cron.sh (updated to include multiple fallback methods)
- Executed /workspace/setup_cron.sh on this system
  - Result: the script created /etc/cron.d/auto_backup with this content:
    SHELL=/bin/bash
    PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
    # Auto-backup: uploads /workspace/important every 15 minutes
    */15 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/python3 /workspace/auto_backup.py >> /workspace/auto_backup.log 2>&1
  - This means a system-level cron job was installed (persistent across reboots).
  - Note: this environment does not provide systemctl or init scripts to start daemons, so the cron 
daemon is not controllable here; however the /etc/cron.d entry is present and will be used by cron when 
the system runs cron.

How to verify (on your target machine)
- Confirm cron entry: cat /etc/cron.d/auto_backup
- Confirm crontab (if using user crontab): crontab -l
- Check log output: tail -n 200 /workspace/auto_backup.log
- Run a manual test: /usr/bin/env python3 /workspace/auto_backup.py

Notes and recommendations
- The job is system-level via /etc/cron.d and therefore will be persistent across reboots.
- If you prefer a user-level systemd timer instead (works without root on systems with systemd), re-run 
setup_cron.sh as the desired user; it will attempt the user-timer fallback.
- If requests is not installed on the host Python environment, auto_backup.py will try a one-time pip 
install. If that fails (no network or pip), install requests manually: python3 -m pip install --user 
requests

If you want further enhancements I can:
- Add log rotation for /workspace/auto_backup.log
- Add retention policy for backup archives (keep last N)
- Add optional authentication for the upload endpoint

